  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are <b>The stack uses an S3 bucket that still contains files.</b> and <b>The stack includes an EC2 Security Group that is associated with running EC2 instances.</b></p><p>CloudFormation cannot delete S3 buckets if there are objects inside them, so you need to empty the bucket before deleting the stack. Similarly, Security Groups cannot be deleted if they are attached to active EC2 instances. Resolve such dependencies to allow the stack deletion to proceed.</p><p>The incorrect options are <i>The stack was created using an outdated template version that now prevents its deletion. Support intervention is required.</i> and <i>The stack defines an EC2 instance based on a custom Amazon Machine Image (AMI).</i>. These situations do not inherently prevent stack deletion. Stacks aren’t blocked from deletion due to template versioning, and referencing a custom AMI also does not block deletion unless there are other dependencies. Be sure to review stack resources for dependencies that might interfere with deletions, particularly removable resources like S3 buckets and security groups.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For smooth stack deletion, always detach or delete resources that cannot be removed by CloudFormation automatically, such as buckets with objects or security groups in use.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization maintains several AWS CloudFormation stacks. During a regular cleanup process, certain stacks have been scheduled for removal. However, some stacks encounter failures and cannot be deleted. What are two plausible reasons why deletion attempts would fail?</p>",
